{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And he said to him, What have you done, etc.</b> One needs to know the intent of his saying <b>what have you done</b>; and if he means to say, what is the evil deed you have done to us, he should have preceded it with ״what have I sinned against you.״ Perhaps in his saying ״what have you done to us״ before ״what have I sinned against you״ he alluded to him: what good you have done for us, and I repaid you evil — for this you brought [this upon us], etc. And the intention is to convey that no person in the world is deserving of evil as one who repays evil for good; therefore he alluded to him that it would not suffice for Avraham to do evil to them — by doing what he did, intending to destroy them from the world — since there is no existence of evil in the world that could serve as its requital, as he said: ״deeds that ought not to be done,״ etc., unless he had first done good for them and they repaid him evil. And all of the verse is words of rebuke for what he did."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:30.772414457Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}